Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The core problem: all previous attempts enter the constrained span immediately
// and generate only "C" (a tiny span), resulting in 0% accuracy. The failure
// diagnostic says "entered_constrained_mode_too_early" and "tiny_span_dominant".
//
// The LM sees the prompt "acrylates" and immediately enters constrained mode.
// The SMILES parser treats "C" as a complete valid SMILES, so CloseConstrainedSpan
// is called after just one token. The result <<C>> is syntactically valid SMILES
// but not an acrylate.
//
// Root cause: the SMILES grammar parser accepts "C" as complete, so the loop
// exits immediately. We need to:
// 1. NOT use visible delimiters (the task says "hidden constrained chunk").
//    The failure says "answer_source: none" -- the hidden extractor found nothing.
//    Wait -- it says "has_extracted_answer: yes" and "last_visible_span 0/50,
//    text_fallback 0/50, hidden_or_task_extractor 0/50". So the span IS being
//    extracted but it's "C" which is not an acrylate.
//
// 2. The key fix: avoid closing the span when the SMILES is too short. But we
//    CANNOT call AppendConstrainedToken when IsCompletePrefix is true!
//
// 3. The real solution: use ConstrainedGeneration which runs until EOS or budget
//    exhaustion, returning the full generated prefix. This bypasses the
//    OpenConstrainedSpan / CloseConstrainedSpan pattern entirely.
//
// 4. BUT: ConstrainedGeneration starts from an EMPTY constrained prefix, so
//    we enter hidden constrained mode (no visible delimiters). The task says
//    "Use the hidden parser-guided constrained chunk". So we should NOT use
//    OpenConstrainedSpan (which adds "<<").
//
// 5. ConstrainedGeneration returns (constrainedGenerated, terminatedByEos).
//    The `constrainedGenerated` is the full SMILES string. We append it directly
//    to `generated` without any delimiters.
//
// 6. Strong guidance to bias toward acrylate SMILES (C=CC(=O)O pattern).
//
// 7. Budget: ConstrainedGeneration uses up to maxSteps steps. Reserve 1 for EOS.
//
// This approach avoids the "tiny span" problem because ConstrainedGeneration
// keeps generating until EOS or budget, not stopping at the first complete prefix.
//
// Note: ConstrainedGeneration requires starting from an empty prefix and uses
// its own internal loop. The cost is the number of tokens generated.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: We do not enter a constrained span (insideConstrainedOut
//   starts false and stays false throughout). The implication
//   `insideConstrainedOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut)` is
//   vacuously true at all times. No calls modify insideConstrainedOut or
//   currentConstrainedOut.
//
// progress: `|generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps`
//   ConstrainedGeneration is called with budget maxSteps and consumes at most
//   maxSteps token steps. We set cost := maxSteps as an upper bound (actual
//   cost is the length of constrainedGenerated plus possibly 1 for EOS).
//   We append constrainedGenerated to generated. Since |constrainedGenerated|
//   <= maxSteps, |generated| = |generatedPrefix| + |constrainedGenerated|
//   <= |generatedPrefix| + maxSteps. The invariant |generated| <= |generatedPrefix|
//   + steps holds with steps := maxSteps after the call.
//   The progress postcondition (maxSteps == 0 || cost > 0 || ...) holds because
//   when maxSteps > 0 we set cost := maxSteps > 0.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
generated := generatedPrefix;
insideConstrainedOut := insideConstrained;
currentConstrainedOut := currentConstrained;
cost := 0;

// Strong guidance to bias the LM toward valid acrylate SMILES strings.
// Acrylates contain C=CC(=O)O (acrylate ester) or C=CC(=O)N (acrylamide).
// Examples: C=CC(=O)OCC, C=CC(=O)OCCO, C=CC(=O)OC(C)C, CCOC(=O)C=C,
//           C=CC(=O)OCC(CC)CCCC, C=C(C)C(=O)OCCCO
var guidance: string := "Output a single valid SMILES string for a novel acrylate molecule. Acrylates contain the substructure C=CC(=O)O (ester) or C=CC(=O)N (amide). The SMILES must start with C=C or end with OC(=O)C=C pattern. Examples of valid acrylate SMILES: C=CC(=O)OCC, C=CC(=O)OCCO, C=CC(=O)OC(C)C, CCOC(=O)C=C, C=CC(=O)OCC(CC)CCCC. Output ONLY the SMILES string.";
helpers.AppendTaskGuidance(lm, guidance);

// If we're already in a constrained span from the caller, we need to handle it.
// But the precondition says !insideConstrained ==> currentConstrained == [],
// and we don't expect to be called with insideConstrained = true for this task.
// We use ConstrainedGeneration to generate hidden SMILES without visible delimiters.

if maxSteps == 0 {
  cost := 0;
} else {
  // Use ConstrainedGeneration for hidden constrained SMILES output.
  // This generates tokens until EOS or budget exhaustion, without visible delimiters.
  // The constrained generation produces a parser-valid SMILES string.
  var constrainedGenerated, terminatedByEos := helpers.ConstrainedGeneration(
    lm, parser, prompt, maxSteps, eosToken
  );

  // Append the generated SMILES to the output (hidden, no delimiters).
  generated := generatedPrefix + constrainedGenerated;

  // Cost is the full budget; actual cost is |constrainedGenerated| + (1 if EOS).
  // We set cost conservatively to maxSteps to satisfy `cost <= maxSteps`.
  // But we need cost > 0 when maxSteps > 0 (progress postcondition).
  // Since maxSteps > 0 and we called ConstrainedGeneration, at least 1 step consumed.
  if |constrainedGenerated| > 0 {
    cost := |constrainedGenerated|;
    if terminatedByEos {
      cost := cost + 1;
    }
    if cost > maxSteps {
      cost := maxSteps;
    }
  } else {
    // Even empty output costs at least 1 step (EOS or empty generation).
    cost := 1;
  }
}

