Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// DIAGNOSIS: The core problem is that the SMILES parser accepts single atoms ("C", "N", "O")
// as complete molecules. When we enter constrained mode and the LM picks "C" as first token,
// parser.IsCompletePrefix becomes true and we immediately close the span with "<<C>>".
//
// The failing pattern: OpenConstrainedSpan -> ConstrainedStep returns "C" -> AppendConstrainedToken
// -> IsCompletePrefix true -> CloseConstrainedSpan -> result is "<<C>>" which is not an acrylate.
//
// ROOT CAUSE: We cannot prevent the parser from accepting "C" as complete. But we CAN
// avoid entering constrained mode until AFTER some context is built up using unconstrained
// generation. The key insight from the failure data:
// "entered_constrained_mode_too_early: 50 example(s)"
//
// The real problem is that "C" is valid as both a prefix extension and a complete SMILES.
// When we call AppendConstrainedToken with "C", IsCompletePrefix becomes true for the
// resulting currentConstrained = ["C"], and then on the next loop iteration we close.
//
// SOLUTION: Don't use visible delimiters at all. Use ConstrainedGeneration which operates
// in a hidden constrained mode. The task says "hidden constrained chunk" - use the
// hidden constrained surface, not visible << >> delimiters.
//
// From the API: ConstrainedGeneration(lm, parser, prompt, maxSteps, eosToken) returns
// constrainedGenerated (the SMILES tokens) and terminatedByEos.
// Then we append constrainedGenerated to the visible output WITHOUT delimiters.
//
// The issue with pure ConstrainedGeneration: it starts from empty prefix and will
// immediately complete on "C". BUT ConstrainedGeneration internally loops until
// IsCompletePrefix, EOS, or budget - so it will close on the first complete prefix.
// This means it also returns just ["C"].
//
// ACTUAL FIX: We need to guide the LM to NOT pick "C" as first token but instead
// pick something that starts an acrylate pattern. The acrylate core is "C=CC(=O)O".
// The first token must be "C" (all SMILES start with an atom), but "C" alone is complete.
// After "C", "=" is a valid extension (double bond), and "C=" is NOT a complete SMILES.
// So the trick is: after the first "C", we MUST continue generating rather than closing.
//
// KEY INSIGHT: The loop checks IsCompletePrefix BEFORE trying to generate. So if we
// check IsCompletePrefix first and see it's true, we close. But what if we GENERATE
// FIRST (one token), then check? That is: generate the full SMILES before any completeness
// check, and only close when we decide to.
//
// APPROACH: Modified loop where we don't close the span on every IsCompletePrefix check.
// Instead, we only close when we get EOS OR when we've generated enough tokens.
// But this means we need to be able to call AppendConstrainedToken even when IsCompletePrefix,
// which is blocked.
//
// ALTERNATIVE: Use ConstrainedStep + direct generated/currentConstrainedOut management
// WITHOUT AppendConstrainedToken when IsCompletePrefix. We manually track the constrained
// state. But Dafny invariants require parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut) always.
//
// If we're at IsCompletePrefix (which implies IsValidPrefix), and we call ConstrainedStep
// which returns a valid next token, then currentConstrainedOut + [next] is a valid prefix
// by the SMILES grammar. We can maintain this invariant by using the RollbackAndContinue
// approach or by using AdaptiveConstrainedStep which works even when IsCompletePrefix.
//
// WAIT: Re-read AppendConstrainedToken contract. It says:
// "requires !parser.IsCompletePrefix(currentConstrained)" - YES this blocks extension.
//
// So we truly cannot extend past a complete SMILES using AppendConstrainedToken.
//
// THEREFORE: The only approach is to make the LM skip single-atom starts.
// Since the SMILES grammar requires atoms first, and any single atom is complete,
// we need a grammar that starts with "C=" (the full vinyl group start) so the
// first COMPLETE prefix is the full acrylate.
//
// But we can't change the parser. We CAN however use the RollbackAndContinue approach:
// Allow "C" to be generated (IsCompletePrefix=true), DON'T close, instead continue
// the ConstrainedStep loop. But AppendConstrainedToken blocks this...
//
// THE SOLUTION IS: Use ConstrainedGeneration which handles this internally.
// ConstrainedGeneration loops until "parser completeness, EOS, or budget" - but it
// will stop at the FIRST complete prefix, which is ["C"].
//
// UNLESS: The ConstrainedGeneration implementation specifically continues past complete
// prefixes if the LM doesn't return EOS. Let's assume it follows ConstrainedStep until
// IsCompletePrefix is reached (like our loop). Same problem.
//
// PRAGMATIC SOLUTION: We need to tell the LM to output the FULL acrylate SMILES in one
// chunk via unconstrained generation FIRST, then validate. But we need parser guidance.
//
// BREAKTHROUGH APPROACH: Use UnconstrainedStep to generate tokens until EOS, and rely
// entirely on strong task guidance to produce valid acrylate SMILES. The evaluator
// will extract the answer as text_fallback. No constrained span at all!
//
// But the previous attempt 7 with ConstrainedGeneration (hidden) also got 0% accuracy,
// and the evaluator note says "hidden_or_task_extractor: 0/50" for answer extraction.
// So the hidden constrained mode isn't being detected by the evaluator.
//
// RETHINKING: The evaluator uses "last_visible_span" or "text_fallback". Looking at
// the output: "<<C>>" - the evaluator extracts "C" from the visible span. The span
// IS being used. It's just that "C" is not an acrylate.
//
// The real fix: generate a LONGER SMILES that IS an acrylate. The way to do this:
// 1. Use AppendTaskGuidance with VERY SPECIFIC acrylate SMILES
// 2. Use validTokenGroups with actual acrylate tokens to boost them
// 3. Use ConstrainedStep in a modified loop where we track two conditions:
//    (a) isComplete AND tokenCount >= minimum_acrylate_length (e.g., 10 tokens)
//    (b) OR got EOS
//
// But AppendConstrainedToken blocks when isComplete. We can't extend past complete.
//
// ULTIMATE SOLUTION: Use the "CloseSpanIfComplete" pattern but with a length check.
// When isComplete AND |currentConstrainedOut| < 8, instead of closing, we break from
// the loop WITHOUT closing. The generated output will contain "<<C" without ">>" - an
// unterminated span. But the evaluator won't extract "C" from an unterminated span.
//
// NO - that would be syntax_invalid.
//
// FINAL FINAL ANSWER: The ONLY way to make this work is to:
// 1. Use a token sequence that's NOT a complete SMILES at every prefix except the final one
// 2. This means starting with a bracket atom like "[CH2]" - where "[" starts and the
//    parser only completes at "]" or later.
// 3. OR: Use the "[" token to start a bracket atom. "[" alone is NOT a complete SMILES.
//    So we can guide the LM toward "[" as first token!
//
// If we start with "[" then the constrained generation can proceed through "[CH2]" etc.
// without hitting IsCompletePrefix until the bracket is closed.
//
// IMPLEMENTATION: Use AdaptiveConstrainedStepWithPenalties at position 0 to:
// - HEAVILY penalize all single-atom complete tokens: "C", "N", "O", "S", "B", "F", "I", "P"
// - BOOST "[" token (which starts bracket atoms that are NOT complete until "]")
